1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,679
Welcome to Atomic Education.

2
00:00:02,739 --> 00:00:09,993
This lesson was written by the Atomic teaching team to help you learn Cambridge Lower Secondary Science with confidence.

3
00:00:09,993 --> 00:00:10,712
Let's begin!

4
00:00:11,772 --> 00:00:14,410
Welcome to Unit 4: The Structure of an Atom.

5
00:00:15,470 --> 00:00:18,408
Everything in the Universe is made of particles.

6
00:00:18,408 --> 00:00:22,185
Atoms are the smallest particles of an element that can exist.

7
00:00:22,185 --> 00:00:27,101
The ancient Greeks called them 'atomos', meaning uncuttable. But were they right?

8
00:00:27,101 --> 00:00:28,720
Let's look inside the atom.

9
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:32,358
Think of atoms as plastic building blocks.

10
00:00:32,358 --> 00:00:36,974
Each element is made of identical atoms of one type, like one kind of block.

11
00:00:36,974 --> 00:00:41,650
A hydrogen atom is like a one-stud block, and helium is like a two-stud block.

12
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:46,367
Atoms of different elements have different sizes and masses.

13
00:00:46,367 --> 00:00:50,804
And just as a few types of block can build houses, cars and whole cities,

14
00:00:50,804 --> 00:00:54,701
a small number of types of atom build everything in the Universe.

15
00:00:55,761 --> 00:00:59,957
If we could open up a helium atom, we'd find it's mostly empty space,

16
00:00:59,957 --> 00:01:02,415
with just three types of particle inside.

17
00:01:03,475 --> 00:01:09,291
At the centre is a tiny nucleus, containing protons, which have a positive charge, and neutrons,

18
00:01:09,291 --> 00:01:13,008
which have no charge. Protons and neutrons have the same mass.

19
00:01:14,068 --> 00:01:17,665
Orbiting the nucleus are electrons, with a negative charge.

20
00:01:17,665 --> 00:01:20,902
They're tiny: about 1836 times lighter than a proton!

21
00:01:20,902 --> 00:01:26,838
An atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons, so the charges balance and the atom is neutral.

22
00:01:27,898 --> 00:01:34,133
A balloon is rubbed on dry hair, then held near a thin stream of water from a tap, without touching it.

23
00:01:34,133 --> 00:01:37,130
Predict: what will the water do? Pause and decide.

24
00:01:38,190 --> 00:01:43,646
Watch. Rubbing moves electrons from the hair onto the balloon, giving it a negative charge.

25
00:01:44,706 --> 00:01:48,723
When the balloon comes close, the stream of water bends towards it.

26
00:01:49,783 --> 00:01:54,819
Explain what you saw. The balloon has extra electrons, so it is negatively charged.

27
00:01:54,819 --> 00:02:00,334
The positive parts of the water are attracted towards it, because opposite charges attract.

28
00:02:00,334 --> 00:02:05,910
This is the same force that holds negative electrons around the positive nucleus in an atom.

29
00:02:05,910 --> 00:02:08,248
Pause and explain it in your own words.

30
00:02:09,308 --> 00:02:13,385
Just like magnets, like charges repel and opposite charges attract.

31
00:02:13,385 --> 00:02:18,181
In an atom, the positive protons in the nucleus pull on the negative electrons.

32
00:02:18,181 --> 00:02:24,536
This pull is the electrostatic force. It acts like an invisible rope, stopping the electrons flying away.

33
00:02:24,536 --> 00:02:29,093
Another force, the nuclear force, holds the protons together in the nucleus.

34
00:02:30,152 --> 00:02:35,728
Try it! Rub a balloon on your hair, and some electrons move from your hair onto the balloon,

35
00:02:35,728 --> 00:02:41,903
giving it a negative charge. Hold it near an empty can lying on its side, and the can rolls towards it!

36
00:02:42,963 --> 00:02:48,539
The atomic number is the number of protons in the nucleus. It is different for every element.

37
00:02:49,599 --> 00:02:54,275
Hydrogen has 1 proton, helium 2, carbon 6, nitrogen 7, oxygen 8 and sodium 11.

38
00:02:55,335 --> 00:02:58,692
Pause and decide: a neutral sodium atom has 11 protons.

39
00:02:58,692 --> 00:03:02,829
How many electrons does it have, and how are they arranged in shells?

40
00:03:03,889 --> 00:03:09,105
Atoms of different elements have different numbers of protons, neutrons and electrons.

41
00:03:09,105 --> 00:03:14,321
Hydrogen has 1 proton and 1 electron. Helium has 2 protons, 2 neutrons and 2 electrons.

42
00:03:15,381 --> 00:03:20,597
Carbon has 6 of each, nitrogen 7, and oxygen 8. Electrons move in paths called shells.

43
00:03:20,597 --> 00:03:24,733
The first shell holds up to 2 electrons, and the next holds up to 8.

44
00:03:24,733 --> 00:03:28,930
So carbon has 2 electrons in its inner shell and 4 in its outer shell.

45
00:03:29,990 --> 00:03:32,628
The number of protons is the atomic number.

46
00:03:32,628 --> 00:03:37,604
It decides which element an atom is, and it also tells us the number of electrons.

47
00:03:37,604 --> 00:03:42,880
Each element also has its own symbol: carbon is C, but chlorine is Cl, and helium is He.

48
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:50,415
Atoms are most stable when their outer shell is full: 2 electrons in the first shell, or 8 in later shells.

49
00:03:50,415 --> 00:03:55,331
A hydrogen atom has just 1 electron, so two hydrogen atoms share their electrons,

50
00:03:55,331 --> 00:04:00,007
and both get a full shell. Together they form a stable hydrogen molecule, H2.

51
00:04:00,007 --> 00:04:03,724
Two nitrogen atoms share electrons in the same way to make N2.

52
00:04:04,784 --> 00:04:07,602
Carbon has 4 outer electrons and needs 4 more,

53
00:04:07,602 --> 00:04:11,379
so it bonds easily with other carbon atoms and other elements.

54
00:04:11,379 --> 00:04:14,856
That's why carbon is the building block of living things.

55
00:04:14,856 --> 00:04:20,312
Carbon atoms can have different numbers of neutrons: carbon-12 has 6, but carbon-14 has 8.

56
00:04:20,312 --> 00:04:26,427
These are called isotopes, and carbon-14 helps scientists find the age of things that were once alive.

57
00:04:27,487 --> 00:04:30,904
Sodium has 11 electrons, with just 1 in its outer shell.

58
00:04:30,904 --> 00:04:36,300
That electron is held weakly, so sodium loses it easily, which makes sodium very reactive.
